   {[}Anonymous], FUTURE DIRECTIONS Important next steps to build on the findings presented here include further use of cross-technique comparisons for the exploration of smectite hydration. Repetition of techniques such as TGA-MS and 23Na NMR alongside RH-controlled XRD with additional swelling clays and alternate cation loadings would be insightful. Further probing of organic structures adsorbed to smectite clays including the introduction of unique functional groups and direct stereochemistry investigation, parallel to controlled moisture conditions would build on the knowledge presented here. Determination of the impact of carbohydrates synthesized for exact chain length at different orders of magnitude would be telling. Future directions may also include probing the potential for mineral-facilitated cleavage of carbohydrates. To understand better the conformations and interactions implicated in these exciting carbohydrate structure-dependent findings, molecular modeling of the systems would be beneficial. The study of flocculation and aggregation in suspensions of MONT loaded with different carbohydrate structures could also be an area of future study. Overall, the above-mentioned subject areas, as well as further points of consideration presented in Chapters 1-3, exist for further contribution to the organo-mineral field in years to come. ACKNOWLEDGMENTS Firstly, thank you to Dr. Ludmilla Aristilde for all the past and present kindness, guidance, support, scientific insight, and motivation you have furnished me with during my graduate tenure. I have truly grown both personally and as a researcher under your tutelage. I want to express huge gratitude to my committee members, Dr. Murray McBride and Dr. Taryn Bauerle for their insights and intellectual contributions that have shaped this work. To Dr. Randall Youngman at Corning Inc, thank you for always being willing to go above and beyond to test a new idea or to look into a new project. There always seemed to b